(8. Event architecture conclusion) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For Syrka, event architecture is the decisive criterion. The selected foundation must emit ) Tj
T*
(granular, semantically useful educational events and permit clean interception without ) Tj
T*
(modifying every activity implementation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Required event families:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(- Identity and enrolment events.) Tj
T*
(- Course, unit, module, and learning-path progress events.) Tj
T*
(- Assignment creation, submission, resubmission, grading, rubric, and feedback events.) Tj
T*
(- Quiz/problem attempt events including timing, correctness, hints, retries, and mastery.) Tj
T*
(- Discussion, peer review, team, collaboration, and research events.) Tj
T*
(- File, portfolio, repository, external tool, and project artifact events.) Tj
T*
(- Attendance and synchronous participation events.) Tj
T*
(- Credential, certificate, badge, and verification events.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Ranking for Syrka event architecture:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Open edX - strongest because of event tracking heritage, XBlock runtime, LTI ) Tj
T*
(compatibility, and Python ecosystem.) Tj
T*
(2. Canvas - excellent APIs and event possibilities, but hosted/self-hosted differences and ) Tj
T*
(Rails monolith concerns.) Tj
T*
(3. Moodle - mature event API and plugins, but less AI-native architecture.) Tj
T*
(4. Sakai - service/tool events are useful, but modernization cost is higher.) Tj
T*
(5. ILIAS - strong permissions/repository model, less natural event-stream architecture.) Tj
T*
(6. Chamilo - simplest, but least capable for deep capability inference.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
